**Flexbox** = one-dimensional layout (row OR column).
**Grid** = two-dimensional layout (rows AND columns).

**Flexbox (best for component layout):**
```css
.navbar {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;  /* Horizontal distribution */
    align-items: center;             /* Vertical alignment */
    gap: 16px;
}

.card-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;  /* Wrap to next line if needed */
    gap: 24px;
}
.card-row > * {
    flex: 1 1 300px;  /* Grow, shrink, minimum 300px */
}
```

**Grid (best for page layout):**
```css
.page {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 250px 1fr;  /* Sidebar + content */
    grid-template-rows: 60px 1fr 40px; /* Header + content + footer */
    min-height: 100vh;
}

.dashboard {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(300px, 1fr));
    gap: 24px;
}
```

**When to use which:**
- Navbar, button group, card content → **Flexbox**
- Page layout, dashboard grid, image gallery → **Grid**
- Both work for most cases — Grid is more powerful, Flexbox is simpler for 1D

**Mobile-first approach:** design for mobile, then add complexity for larger screens.

```css
/* Mobile first (default styles) */
.container {
    padding: 16px;
    max-width: 1200px;
    margin: 0 auto;
}

.grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;  /* Single column on mobile */
    gap: 16px;
}

/* Tablet (768px+) */
@media (min-width: 768px) {
    .container { padding: 24px; }
    .grid {
        grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);  /* 2 columns */
    }
}

/* Desktop (1024px+) */
@media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .container { padding: 32px; }
    .grid {
        grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);  /* 3 columns */
    }
}
```

**Key techniques:**
- Use `rem`/`em` not `px` for font sizes (respects user preferences)
- Use `%` or `vw` for widths (adapts to screen)
- Use `max-width` not `width` (prevents horizontal scroll)
- Use `clamp()` for fluid typography: `font-size: clamp(1rem, 2.5vw, 2rem)`
- Test on real devices, not just browser resize

**Essential meta tag:**
```html
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
```
Without this, mobile browsers render at desktop width and zoom out.

CSS custom properties (variables) let you define reusable values and change them dynamically.

```css
:root {
    --bg: #0a0a0a;
    --text: #d8d8d8;
    --primary: #3b82f6;
    --radius: 8px;
    --spacing: 16px;
}

.card {
    background: var(--bg);
    color: var(--text);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: var(--spacing);
}

.button {
    background: var(--primary);
    padding: calc(var(--spacing) / 2) var(--spacing);
}

/* Dark/light theme with just variable changes */
[data-theme='light'] {
    --bg: #ffffff;
    --text: #1a1a1a;
}

/* Component-level override */
.sidebar {
    --spacing: 12px;  /* Narrower spacing in sidebar */
}
```

**Dynamic with JavaScript:**
```javascript
// Set variable
document.documentElement.style.setProperty('--primary', '#ef4444');

// Read variable
getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue('--primary');
```

CSS variables cascade like any other property. Define globally in `:root`, override locally in components. This is how modern CSS theming works — change a few variables, the entire site updates instantly.

Two approaches: `transition` (simple state changes) and `@keyframes` (complex animations).

**Transition (hover, focus, state changes):**
```css
.button {
    background: #3b82f6;
    transform: scale(1);
    transition: background 0.2s ease, transform 0.2s ease;
}

.button:hover {
    background: #2563eb;
    transform: scale(1.05);
}
```

**Keyframes (complex, multi-step):**
```css
@keyframes fadeIn {
    from {
        opacity: 0;
        transform: translateY(20px);
    }
    to {
        opacity: 1;
        transform: translateY(0);
    }
}

.card {
    animation: fadeIn 0.3s ease-out;
}

@keyframes spin {
    to { transform: rotate(360deg); }
}

.spinner {
    animation: spin 1s linear infinite;
}
```

**Performance tip:** Only animate `transform` and `opacity` — they run on the GPU (compositor layer) and don't trigger layout/paint. Animating `width`, `height`, `top`, `left` causes layout recalculation every frame and will be janky.

```css
/* BAD (triggers layout): */
.box { left: 0; transition: left 0.3s; }
.box:hover { left: 100px; }

/* GOOD (GPU-accelerated): */
.box { transform: translateX(0); transition: transform 0.3s; }
.box:hover { transform: translateX(100px); }
```
